AB  - Cocoa production is a key economic driver for many countries, such as Côte d’Ivoire and Ghana, making optimal harvesting crucial for yield and quality. Therefore, by automating the ripeness detection process, our approach reduces human dependency and enhances harvesting efficiency. This study explores machine learning and deep learning approaches for the detection of cocoa pod ripeness using images. For the machine learning pipeline, we preprocess images and extract features using Gray-Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM) and Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT). The features are then used for training a classifier to classify between mature and unmature cocoa pods. We compare its performance against deep learning models, namely, VGG16, ResNet50, and YOLOv8. We utilize CocoaMFDB, a dataset of enhanced cocoa pod images from Côte d’Ivoire, as training data. The trained models achieve a high accuracy of 99.19% with YOLOv8 followed by 97.61% with ResNet50, demonstrating their potential for practical deployment in cocoa farms.
